<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Alcoa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1888">1888</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Aluminum Company of America</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Alcoa, the Aluminum Company of America, is an American aluminium producer founded in Pittsburgh in 1888 to exploit Charles Martin Hall's process for refining aluminium. Through its subsidiary Suralco it mined bauxite in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suriname">Suriname</a> from the early twentieth century, starting at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moengo">Moengo</a>, and later built the Paranam smelter powered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afobaka-dam">Afobaka Dam</a>, making bauxite the backbone of the Surinamese economy for decades.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alcoa.jpg" alt="Alcoa" width="320" height="288" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alcoa · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Alcoa_logo_(2016).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>In 1886 Charles Martin Hall, a graduate of Oberlin College, found that passing an electric current through a bath of cryolite and aluminium oxide produced aluminium; Paul Héroult in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> made the same discovery almost at once. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hall-heroult-process">Hall–Héroult process</a> cut production costs at a time when aluminium sold for almost five dollars a pound, more than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> or gold, and fewer than ten sites in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> produced it.</p><p>Hall left a smelting company in Lockport, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-york">New York</a>, which did not use his process, and joined Alfred E. Hunt. Arthur Vining Davis joined in 1888, and their first commercial pour took place on Thanksgiving Day that year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Pittsburgh Reduction Company began with an experimental plant on Smallman Street in Pittsburgh, with Hunt as president and Hall as vice president. It began production at New Kensington, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pennsylvania">Pennsylvania</a> in 1891 and opened a plant at Niagara Falls in 1895. Hunt left in 1898 to fight in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a>, caught malaria in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/puerto-rico">Puerto Rico</a> and died of its complications at the age of 44.</p><p>By about 1903 the company was the only legal supplier of aluminium in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. It became the Aluminum Company of America on 1 January 1907, and the acronym Alcoa was coined when Davis became president in 1910. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> it raised production by 40% and exported about ninety million pounds to the Western Allies.</p><p>In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">World War II</a>, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">World War I</a>, about 90% of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> aluminium went to military uses. In 1938 the Justice Department charged Alcoa with illegal monopolization and demanded that it be dissolved, and the case, United States v. Alcoa, was settled six years later. Davis was chairman of the board from 1928 for thirty years. Alcoa bought Alumax in 1998, Reynolds Metals in 2000 and Cordant Technologies in 2000. In 2014 it settled with United States authorities over charges of bribing Bahraini officials.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Alcoa gave its name to places where it built facilities, including a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tennessee">Tennessee</a>. It sponsored television anthology series from 1955 to 1963, among them The Alcoa Hour on NBC. On 1 November 2016 Alcoa Inc. split into two companies. The new Alcoa Corporation kept the mining and manufacture of raw aluminium, and the old company was renamed Arconic Inc., which processes aluminium and other metals. Alcoa Corporation produces aluminium in ten countries.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Alcoa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
